  "answer_text": "The standard and most widely accepted way to pronounce **doge** (as in the meme and the cryptocurrency Dogecoin) is:\n\n### **\u201cDOHJ\u201d** (rhymes with \"vogue\" but ends with a soft \"j\" sound)\n* **Phonetic Spelling:** `/do\u028ad\u0292/`\n* **How to say it:** Start by saying **\"go\"** or **\"no\"** to get that long \"O\" sound. Then, add the soft \"j\" sound at the end (like the \"j\" in *judge* or *jam*). \n* *Think of it as the word \"Dodge,\" but with a long \"O\" (like \"doh-j\").*\n\n---\n\n### **The Acceptable Alternative: \u201cDOHZH\u201d**\n* **Phonetic Spelling:** `/do\u028a\u0292/`\n* **How to say it:** This is almost identical to \"dohj,\" but instead of a hard \"j\" sound, it ends with a soft, buzzing \"zh\" sound (like the \"S\" in *treasure*, *measure*, or *casual*, or the French pronunciation of \"je\"). Many news anchors, crypto enthusiasts, and the creators of Dogecoin use this pronunciation as well.\n\n---\n\n### **Why is this the \"official\" pronunciation?**\nThe intentional misspelling of \"dog\" as \"D-O-G-E\" dates back to a June 2005 episode of the internet puppet show ***Homestar Runner***. In the video, Homestar spells out \"D-O-G-E\" while trying to talk to Strong Bad, to which Strong Bad replies in confusion: *\"Your **doge [dohj]**? Rocketman, you crack me up\"*. \n\nWhen the Shiba Inu meme exploded years later and ultimately inspired Dogecoin, the community adopted Strong Bad's original oral pronunciation.\n\nIt also happens to align with the historical pronunciation of **Doge**, the title used for the elected rulers of Venice and Genoa during the Middle Ages and Renaissance.\n\n---\n\n### **Common \"Wrong\" Pronunciations**\nWhile the internet is a free space and there is no \"pronunciation police,\" you will likely be corrected by internet/crypto circles if you say:\n* **\"DOG-ee\"** (like *doggy*) \u2014 Though it refers to a dog, pronouncing it like a normal \"doggy\" defeats the purpose of the spelling.\n* **\"DOHG\"** (rhyming with *vogue* or *rogue*, with a hard \"G\").\n* **\"DODGE\"** (like the car brand or *dodgeball*) \u2014 Common, but incorrect because of the short \"O\" sound.",
